$bog_smalljs_versus $mol_view
	sub /
		<= Head $mol_view
			sub /
				<= Title $mol_view
					dom_name \h1
					sub /
						<= title_text @ \Same code, different behaviour
				<= Intro $mol_view
					sub /
						<= intro_text @ \Four everyday situations, each running side by side in React, Vue and $mol. Press Run on a case and watch all three frames at once: the code executes in your browser, nothing here is recorded in advance. The React and Vue versions are written the way their default templates are written, and every frame notes what you would add by hand to avoid what you just saw.
		<= Cases $mol_view
			sub <= cases /
		<= Method $mol_view
			sub /
				<= Method_title $mol_view
					dom_name \h2
					sub /
						<= method_title_text @ \How this is measured
				<= Method_text $mol_view
					sub /
						<= method_body @ \Every case is one scenario written three times, with the same steps and the same timings in each frame. Counters are read inside the frame right after the run and reported as they come, without rounding or averaging across attempts. The sources of every scenario live in the repository, so you can read them, run them locally, or open an issue if a case looks unfair.
				<= Method_link $mol_link
					uri \https://github.com/b-on-g/smalljs/tree/master/versus
					sub /
						<= method_link_label @ \Scenario sources on GitHub
						<= Method_link_icon $mol_icon_open_in_new
	Case_race $bog_smalljs_versus_case
		case_id \race
		title @ \Stale response overwrites fresh one
		hint @ \Click through a list faster than the server answers.
	Case_virtual $bog_smalljs_versus_case
		case_id \virtual
		title @ \10,000 rows of irregular height
		hint @ \Scroll a long list where every row has a different height.
	Case_leak $bog_smalljs_versus_case
		case_id \leak
		title @ \Subscriptions after 100 mount cycles
		hint @ \Mount and unmount a subscribed component a hundred times.
	Case_crash $bog_smalljs_versus_case
		case_id \crash
		title @ \One broken item takes down the tree
		hint @ \Put malformed data into one card out of twenty.
